# Line endings — preserving byte-exactness for the generated artifacts.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (measured 2026-10-12). `dist/vera-mcp.mjs` is served verbatim at
# GET /mcp.mjs and advertised with a sha256, and `worker/mcp-bundle.js` is the string the
# Worker returns. With `core.autocrlf=true` — which is set on the authoring machine — git
# converts LF to CRLF on CHECKOUT for files it considers text. A fresh clone would then
# receive different bytes for the same commit: the published digest would not match the
# delivered file, and `node tools/build-standalone.mjs --check` would fail for everyone
# except the person who generated it. That failure does not appear locally, which is what
# makes it worth a rule rather than a note.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: in gitattributes the LAST matching rule wins for each attribute, so the
# wildcard must come FIRST and the specific exemptions AFTER it. Written the other way
# round — specific rules first — the wildcard silently overrides them and the exemption
# does nothing, which is exactly what happened on the first attempt here
# (`git ls-files --eol` reported attr/text=auto eol=lf, not attr/-text).

# Default: one canonical form in the repository, LF, no per-machine surprises.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `-text` disables every end-of-line conversion for exactly these paths. Verified with
# `git ls-files --eol` after `git add --renormalize .` — it must report `attr/-text`.
dist/vera-mcp.mjs     -text
worker/mcp-bundle.js  -text
worker/frame-bundle.js -text
